{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["The utterance of Avram the prisoner of hope, who opened the eyes of Jethro." "My witness — half his heart went, and answered the restlessness of Jethro." "And this is the interpretation of the section, at its beginning — the matter of Jethro." "<b>And Jethro heard.</b> Above, the matter of Amalek was mentioned, for [he came] at Rephidim (above 17:8), and the section \"In the third month\" (19:1) ought to have been written after the matter of Amalek, for there it is written \"and they journeyed from Rephidim and came to the wilderness of Sinai\" (ibid. v. 2). Why then was the matter of Jethro inserted between the two sections? And the Gaon [Saadia] said that Jethro came to the wilderness of Sinai before the giving of the Torah. In my opinion he did not come until the second year, after the Tabernacle was erected, for it is written in the section \"and he offered burnt-offerings and sacrifices to Elohim\" (below v. 12), and it does not mention that he built a new altar. Furthermore it is written \"and I make known the statutes of Elohim and His teachings\" (v. 16) — and this is after the giving of the Torah. And the reliable witness to my words is that it is written, \"to the wilderness where he encamps there, the mountain of Elohim\" (v. 5), and Moses said to Hobab, who is Jethro, as I explained in the section of 'Ve-eleh Shemot' — that Moses's father-in-law did not return to Reuel, who was his relative, but to Hobab, for thus it is written \"of the sons of Hobab, Moses's father-in-law\" (Judges 4:11). And Moses said to him, \"for you know where we encamp in the wilderness\" (Num. 10:31). We know that Israel stood in the wilderness of Sinai about a year, and Moses's words prove it, for he said in the section 'Eleh HaDevarim': \"YHWH our Elohim spoke to us at Horeb, saying: You have dwelt long enough at this mountain; turn and journey for yourselves\" (Deut. 1:6–7). And this time was close to their journeying, and he said, \"And I spoke to you at that time, saying: I am unable to bear you alone\" (ibid. v. 9), for HaShem has multiplied you, and here you are today as the stars of the heavens for multitude (ibid. v. 10). And the reason to mention this to them is that HaShem said to Jacob, \"Behold, I will make you fruitful and multiply you, and I will make you a congregation of nations, and I will give this land to your seed after you\" (Gen. 48:4). And the meaning is: when your seed multiplies I will give them the land; therefore YHWH your Elohim has multiplied you, and the time has come for you to inherit the land, and because you are many, I could not bear you alone, and I needed to appoint over you officers of thousands and officers of hundreds. This was the advice that Jethro gave him the day after they came to the wilderness of Sinai; therefore Moses said to Hobab, who is Jethro, as we explained, \"We are journeying\" (Num. 10:29), and he answered, \"I will not go, but to my land and to my kindred I will go\" (ibid. v. 30), and this is \"And Moses sent his father-in-law, and he went to his land\" (v. 27). And now I will explain why the section of Jethro was inserted in this place: because above he mentioned the evil that Amalek did to Israel, he mentioned opposite it the good that Jethro did for Israel, and it is written \"and Jethro rejoiced over all the good,\" and he gave them good and sound advice to Moses and to Israel. And Moses said to him, \"and you have been to us as eyes\" (Num. 10:31), and the meaning is that he illuminated their eyes. And Saul said, \"and you acted with kindness with all the children of Israel\" (1 Sam. 15:6). And because it is written above \"war to YHWH against Amalek\" (17:16) — that Israel are obligated to fight him when HaShem grants them rest — the matter of Jethro was mentioned, for they were with the nation of Amalek, so that Israel would remember the kindness of their father and not touch his seed. And we have seen the Rechabites, who are the children of Jethro, who were with the children of Israel in Jerusalem in the days of Jeremiah, \"Jonadab son of Rechab\" (Jer. 35:19). And there is another section similar to this one:" "<b>That Elohim did.</b> The name that was known in the world before Moses came, in which all acknowledge." "<b>And to Israel His people.</b> The people of YHWH or the people of Moses. And it is possible that the lamed is on account of Moses and Israel, and the meaning concerns the plagues and the drowning of Pharaoh." "<b>For YHWH brought out.</b> The name that became known to Moses, for concerning the signs they went out."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {3 "In the phrase 'they were with the nation of Amalek' (היו עם גוי עמלק), the referent is apparently the Kenites/Jethro's descendants, not Israel; the syntax is compressed and the antecedent is inferred from context."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:41.068959324Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}